    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Traditionally held to be records kept by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s disciples, the Analects is now thought to have been compiled over about two centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of around 300 BCE show that his sayings were being gathered by the mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</li><li>Its sayings teach the cultivation of ren, an all-encompassing virtue, through filial piety and li, ritual propriety, and hold that a ruler should lead by de, moral force and example, rather than by punishment.</li><li>Promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> as one of the Four Books, it was memorised with his commentary by all who sought office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until 1905, and its ideas still influence East Asian thought and values.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Of the sayings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> quoted by his second-generation student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, 75% are not found in the received text of the Analects.</p></aside>
    </section>

