    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> dies; by tradition his disciples then collect and jointly edit their records of his conversations</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The bamboo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> now called Zhongni Said and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Kongzi</a> Said record sayings of Confucius in a format like that of the Analects</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">157–141 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han">Emperor Jing of Han</a> an Old Text version is found hidden in a wall of the house believed to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 50 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingzhou">Dingzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> Analects, two handwritten copies later found in tombs, are written in the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">248 CE</span><span class="rf-text">He Yan's Collected Explanations of the Analects is written, for nearly 1,000 years the standard guide to the text</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">12th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> promotes the Analects as one of the Four Books and writes the commentary that later displaces He Yan's</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th century</span><span class="rf-text">The Ming state endorses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>'s commentary on the Analects</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2011</span><span class="rf-text">The Qi version, lost for about 1,800 years, is found in the tomb of the Marquis of Haihun</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mencius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mencius.jpg" alt="Mencius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Half_Portraits_of_the_Great_Sage_and_Virtuous_Men_of_Old_-_Meng_Ke_(%E5%AD%9F%E8%BB%BB).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> viewed himself as a “transmitter” of social and political traditions that began in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> (c. 1000–800 BCE) and claimed to have originated nothing (§7.1), yet his ideals were not popular in his own time. His political theories ran directly against the Legalist orientation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s rulers, and he failed to popularise them among the leaders of his day. He believed that the social chaos of his age came largely from a ruling elite claiming titles of which they were unworthy. Very few reliable sources about him exist besides the Analects: the principal biography, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>, holds much unverifiable and possibly legendary material, so the Analects is arguably the most reliable source on his life.</p><p>According to Ban Gu, writing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>, the work began as records kept by Confucius's disciples of his conversations with them, collected and jointly edited after his death in 479 BCE. Its title, Lunyu, may mean “edited conversations” or “selected speeches”, hence “analects”. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> likewise called it the records of Confucius's first- and second-generation pupils. A larger body of his teachings existed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> than the Analects preserves: 75% of the sayings of Confucius cited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> are not in the received text, and the Han writer Wang Chong claimed that every Han copy was only part of a much larger work.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/confucius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confucius.jpg" alt="Confucius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius,_fresco_from_a_Western_Han_tomb_of_Dongping_County,_Shandong_province,_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous Chinese painter of the Western Han period, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Analects is a collection of short passages in twenty chapters. The traditional chapter titles are mostly their opening words, the chapters are grouped by individual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> but not arranged as a continuous stream of thought, and central themes recur, sometimes in exactly the same wording. Many passages open with ziyue, “The Master said”, though without punctuation in classical Chinese it is unclear whether a direct quotation or a paraphrase follows. Chapter 10 describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s behaviour in daily activities in detail, and the archaic first verse of Chapter 20, an admonition by Yao to Shun, seems only tangentially related to Confucius.</p><p>At its centre is ren, which Confucius used for an all-encompassing state of virtue that no living person had fully attained; asked about it, he answered with examples rather than definitions. Its most basic step is devotion to one's parents and older siblings, and it is cultivated through the inverted Golden Rule, “Do not do to others what you would not like done to yourself”, and through li, the rituals and forms of propriety by which people show respect for others and their roles in society. A good ruler leads by de, a moral force that wins loyalty without coercion, and by example; good government “consists in the ruler being a ruler, the minister being a minister, the father being a father, and the son being a son”, and a ruler's first task is the rectification of names.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The traditional account has been challenged by Chinese, Japanese and Western scholars. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> philologist Cui Shu argued on linguistic grounds that the last five books were produced much later than the rest; Itō Jinsai distinguished an “upper Analects” (Books 1–10) from a “lower Analects” (Books 11–20); Arthur Waley speculated that Books 3–9 are the earliest parts; and E. Bruce Brooks and A. Taeko Brooks proposed a “four stratum theory”. An “accretion theory” sees the text growing over a 230-year period from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s death to the conquest of Lu in 249 BCE. Many modern scholars believe the work was compiled over about two hundred years during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and some proposed dates as late as 140 BCE for its final compilation.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscripts</a> of the 2010s moved the evidence earlier. Bamboo strips acquired by Anhui University in 2015, the “Zhongni Said”, and the “Kongzi Said” strips recovered from a tomb at Wangjiazui in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> between 2019 and 2021 both date to around 300 BCE, confirming that Confucius's sayings were being preserved and organised by the mid-Warring States period. At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> there were two versions, the Lu of twenty chapters and the Qi of twenty-two, and an Old Text version of twenty-one chapters was later found hidden in a wall of the house believed to have been Confucius's. Zhang Yu, tutor to Emperor Cheng of Han, combined the Lu and Qi versions into the Zhang Hou Lun, the text recognised as the Analects today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhu-xi.jpg" alt="Zhu Xi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%AF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%AF%E9%9D%A0%E7%9A%84%E6%9C%B1%E7%86%B9%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">向史公哲曰, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of the Han period the Analects was not a principal text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>. Under Han Wudi (141–87 BCE), when the government began promoting Confucian studies, only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> were canonical, and the Analects was thought merely a collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s oral commentary on them. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> it was widely read by schoolchildren and anyone aspiring to literacy, often before the Five Classics, and the heir apparent was given a tutor to teach it to him. It joined the Five Classics and the Classic of Filial Piety in the “Seven Classics”, and its style was imitated: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> writer Wang Tong wrote his Explanation of the Mean in its manner.</p><p>Chinese readers have long approached it through commentaries. He Yan's Collected Explanations, written in 248 CE and guided by his belief that Daoism and Confucianism complemented each other, remained the standard guide for nearly 1,000 years and is the oldest complete commentary that survives. It was displaced by the Collected Commentaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> (1130–1200), who read the Analects through the other Four Books and promoted it as one of them, so that by the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> it was generally held more insightful than the Five Classics. The Ming state endorsed Zhu's commentary in the fourteenth century, and until 1905 it was read and memorised with the Analects by all Chinese aspiring to literacy and to employment as government officials.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunhuang.jpg" alt="Dunhuang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiucenglou_of_Mogao_Caves.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">慕尼黑啤酒, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neither the Lu version nor the Old Text version survives complete, though fragments of the Old Text were found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>. According to Huan Tan, the Old Text differed from the Lu by four hundred characters and seriously in 27 places, and the received text agrees with it in only two of them. Before the late twentieth century the oldest known copy was in the Stone Classics of the Xinping Era, the Confucian classics written in stone at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> c. 175 CE. Archaeologists have since found two handwritten copies of about 50 BCE in Western Han tombs: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingzhou">Dingzhou</a> Analects, found in 1973 but not transcribed in print until 1997, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> Analects, found in 1992.</p><p>Both have their difficulties. The Dingzhou copy was damaged by fire soon after it was entombed and by an earthquake soon after its recovery, and the surviving text is just under half the size of the received one; where they overlap it is shorter, which implies that the Analects was still expanding when it was buried. Academic access to the Pyongyang copy has been highly restricted by the North Korean government, and no academic study of it appeared until 2009. The Qi version, lost for about 1,800 years, was found in 2011 in the tomb of the Marquis of Haihun, whose copy circulated at least seventeen years before the other two. Of the Wangjiazui <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> only a third of its 330 strips survive, and it has not yet been published in full.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas de Largillière, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Analects has been one of the most widely read and studied books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for more than two millennia, and its ideas continue to have a substantial influence on East Asian thought and values. It and its commentaries are applied in many cultural expressions across East and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, in China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, above all in education, and it has long influenced traditional East Asian martial arts, where it still shapes practice and teaching. Later Confucian philosophers read its ren as a kind manner, “humane” or “benevolent”, a sense that few of the sixty instances in the text have.</p><p>Writers outside China have found in it a portrait of a person. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and Ezra Pound took Chapter 10 to show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> was a mere human; Simon Leys, its translator into English and French, said that it may have been the first book in history to describe the life of an individual, historic personage; and Elias Canetti called it “the oldest complete intellectual and spiritual portrait of a man”. Its translations run from James Legge's Chinese Classics of 1861 and Arthur Waley's version of 1938 to D. C. Lau's for Penguin in 1979 and Edward Slingerland's of 2003, with the French versions of Séraphin Couvreur and Anne Cheng and Kōjirō Yoshikawa's Japanese Rongo.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Analects gathers short sayings attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and his contemporaries, compiled over generations after his death in 479 BCE and fixed in its received form under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Its teaching of ren, li and rule by virtue made it, once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> named it one of the Four Books, a text memorised by all who sought office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until 1905, and it still influences East Asian thought.</em></p>

